  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Phaeolus hispidoides, commonly known as the New World Dyer's Polypore, is a wood-decay fungus in the Phaeolaceae family that produces distinctive brown fruiting bodies with a velvety surface. This species is notable for its historical and contemporary use by dyers seeking rich brown and golden hues from natural materials.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface Color",
      "description": "Small, angular pores on the underside that are initially pale yellow-brown and darken to rust-brown or cinnamon with age and bruising",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Upper Surface Texture",
      "description": "Velvety, hispid (hairy) brown cap surface that may have a wrinkled or uneven appearance",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Growth Pattern",
      "description": "Bracket-like fruiting bodies that grow on hardwood logs and stumps, often becoming quite thick and shelf-like",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found throughout New England on dead or dying hardwoods, particularly oak and birch, in moist woodlands and forests.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Indigenous peoples and early dyers discovered this polypore produces excellent golden-brown dyes when properly extracted, making it a valuable natural dye source that rivals synthetic colorants.",
